WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.259 --> 00:00:10.620
It’s estimated that seatbelts have collectively
saved over one million lives, making them

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:14.450
one of the most cost-effective public health
inventions ever made.

00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:20.010
[1] These incredible devices can reduce fatalities
in car accidents by about half.

00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:26.109
Yet their development and implementation as
standard equipment was surprisingly rocky.

00:00:26.109 --> 00:00:31.169
For something that seems so straightforward,
the humble seatbelt is more sophisticated

00:00:31.169 --> 00:00:33.120
than you may think.

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:38.450
Even though wearing a restraint in a vehicle
moving 100 kilometers per hour may seem like

00:00:38.450 --> 00:00:40.410
common sense to us today.

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:46.090
It took many years to convince the driving
public to buckle up, a problem that persists

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:48.700
in many countries around the world.

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:58.800
This ingenius device saves lives, and this
is how it does it.

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:06.190
In 1903 race car driver Barney Oldfield became
the first person to drive over 100 km/h.

00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:11.350
He was all too aware of the dangers he was
subjecting himself to and commissioned Leslie

00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:18.360
Leroy Irvin, another dare devil of the 1920s,
to develop a harness for his racing cars.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:24.370
Irvin had developed his own static line parachute
to jump out of airplanes, and 1919 became

00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:30.280
the first American to jump out of a plane
and manually deploy a parachute from a backpack

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:31.280
in midair.

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:32.280
A design he helped the US military to develop.[2]

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:34.930
The harness they came up with did not catch
on however.

00:01:34.930 --> 00:01:38.370
Despite its life saving potential, it had
a number of problems.

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:43.549
First, they were simply uncomfortable, which
discouraged drivers and passengers from wearing

00:01:43.549 --> 00:01:44.549
them.

00:01:44.549 --> 00:01:49.390
Then there was the very real problem of “seat
belt syndrome,” injuries directly related

00:01:49.390 --> 00:01:52.700
to wearing a seatbelt during a collision.

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:55.500
Early seatbelts only went across the lap.

00:01:55.500 --> 00:02:00.509
In the event of a crash, this rigid single
point restraint, laying in line with vital

00:02:00.509 --> 00:02:03.900
organs and the lumbar spine, could cause severe
injuries.

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:04.900
[3]

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:08.580
The prevailing thought at the time was that
it would be better to be ejected from the

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:13.920
vehicle, which might catch on fire or go under
water, than to remain inside of it during

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:18.390
a crash by being stopped forcefully by a thin
piece of fabric.

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:24.230
Despite rising fatalities and injuries during
the rise of the automobile from the 1920s

00:02:24.230 --> 00:02:29.050
to 1950s, the general public showed little
interest in wearing seatbelts. [4]

00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:35.170
Even though Nash Motors installed seat belts
in 40,000 cars, only about 1000 of them were

00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:37.100
actually used by drivers.

00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:42.720
[5] When Ford led a marketing campaign in
1955 to promote seat belts and other safety

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:45.760
options, they too faced public backlash.

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:51.480
However, in the 1950s resistance began to
ebb away with a new innovation by Swedish

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:53.770
engineer Nils Bohlin.

00:02:53.770 --> 00:02:59.510
Bohlin first began developing safety features
for Saab, including an ejection seat, but

00:02:59.510 --> 00:03:02.230
was then poached by the president of Volvo.

00:03:02.230 --> 00:03:07.720
He experimented with several designs, including
a seatbelt that fastened between the legs,

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:13.610
an idea which 95 % of my audience just gasped
at, [6] He ultimately settled on the design

00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:15.610
we’re familiar with today.

00:03:15.610 --> 00:03:17.510
The three point harness.

00:03:17.510 --> 00:03:22.640
This set up more evenly distributes the forces
of a crash across the shoulders and hips.

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:23.640
[7]

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:27.959
Bohlin was able to prove the effectiveness
of this design and received the U.S. patent

00:03:27.959 --> 00:03:29.700
for it in 1962.

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:35.440
But rather than profit from it, the design
was released for free, paving the way for

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:38.310
a new and incredibly important safety feature.

00:03:38.310 --> 00:03:39.310
[8]

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:43.299
While certain elements of the seat belt have
been modified and improved over time, the

00:03:43.299 --> 00:03:45.510
main design remains the same.

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:51.280
We need a way for the strap itself to wind
and unwind under normal circumstances.

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:55.730
Allowing passengers to easily move around,
and take it on and off without adjusting its

00:03:55.730 --> 00:03:58.810
size for different passengers and drivers.

00:03:58.810 --> 00:04:03.810
We have all experienced that annoying phenomenon
where you try to put on a seatbelt too quickly

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:05.480
causing it to jam.

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:10.060
This happens as a result of a false alarm
in the seatbelt mechanism.

00:04:10.060 --> 00:04:11.060
This mechanism.

00:04:11.060 --> 00:04:16.130
It has a number of components, the simplest
being this spiral torsion spring.

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:21.579
When the seatbelt is unwound it spins a cylinder
at the center of this torsion spring, pulling

00:04:21.579 --> 00:04:26.389
the spring into the center into a compressed
state, storing energy that can retract the

00:04:26.389 --> 00:04:31.130
seat belt tightly against your body once the
the belt buckle is locked into the buckle

00:04:31.130 --> 00:04:32.130
socket.

00:04:32.130 --> 00:04:36.530
This mechanism is usually attached to one
side of the webbing spool, while the locking

00:04:36.530 --> 00:04:41.560
mechanism that will prevent the spool from
being unwound at all during a crash is located

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:43.270
on the other side.

00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:48.620
For this mechanism to work we need a way to
detect a crash extremely quickly.

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:53.110
One kind of locking mechanism reacts to the
momentum change of the vehicle.

00:04:53.110 --> 00:04:57.639
Just as our bodies want to continue moving
forward in a crash, a weighted mechanism inside

00:04:57.639 --> 00:04:59.770
the seatbelt will too.

00:04:59.770 --> 00:05:05.210
First we need a ratchet gear, like this, attached
to the same cylinder at the center of our

00:05:05.210 --> 00:05:06.400
torsion spring.

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:09.419
This gear will be engaged to prevent rotation.

00:05:09.419 --> 00:05:14.580
Now we need a mechanism that can engage this
gear when the car decelerates.

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:16.840
This can be done with a weight pendulum.

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:21.740
When the car decelerates this weight will
swing forward, causing its opposite end to

00:05:21.740 --> 00:05:26.479
jam into the ratchet, preventing it from rotating
any further.

00:05:26.479 --> 00:05:31.740
Like any good safety mechanism there is a
redundancy in its design, being backed up

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:33.870
by another locking mechanism.

00:05:33.870 --> 00:05:38.990
One you can test without crashing your car,
by tugging on your seatbelt hard enough this

00:05:38.990 --> 00:05:41.210
mechanism will activate.

00:05:41.210 --> 00:05:44.550
As it depends on centrifugal force to activate.

00:05:44.550 --> 00:05:47.770
Two weighted levers are attached to the central
spool.

00:05:47.770 --> 00:05:52.509
When the central spool unwinds at a normal
speed, these levers do not create enough force

00:05:52.509 --> 00:05:57.060
to overcome the force of a spring that pushes
them towards the center.

00:05:57.060 --> 00:06:02.110
But, if the webbing comes out in a sudden
jerk, the centrifugal force exceeds the force

00:06:02.110 --> 00:06:07.650
of the spring and the levers push outwards
engaging the locking teeth and prevent further

00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:08.800
rotation.

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:13.560
There is another final safety mechanism added
to make seat belts even safer.

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:19.430
When driving we often slump forward slightly,
leaving slack between our body and the seat.

00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:24.440
In the event of a crash it’s best if we
are tightly held against the seat, so there

00:06:24.440 --> 00:06:29.780
is a mechanism within seat belts that actually
forcefully pulls us against the seat during

00:06:29.780 --> 00:06:31.110
a crash.

00:06:31.110 --> 00:06:36.190
This mechanism is fired electronically by
the same sensor that initiates the explosive

00:06:36.190 --> 00:06:41.069
that expands airbags, and this mechanism is
also fired by an explosive charge.

00:06:41.069 --> 00:06:43.250
Animation 6
It’s called the pretensioner.

00:06:43.250 --> 00:06:49.110
A chamber at the bottom of the device has
two electrodes, an igniter material and a

00:06:49.110 --> 00:06:50.110
piston.

00:06:50.110 --> 00:06:54.129
When the car’s central processor detects
a collision, it applies an electric current

00:06:54.129 --> 00:06:59.740
that ignites the explosive and the sudden
burst of pressure forces a mechanism to actuate.

00:06:59.740 --> 00:07:04.840
To quickly retract the seat belt and pull
the passenger tightly against the seat.

00:07:04.840 --> 00:07:11.350
Finally, the seatbelt has several mechanisms
built in to reduce the risk of seat belt syndrome–injuries.

00:07:11.350 --> 00:07:15.820
Some webbing has a fold sewn into it, with
stitches that will break when a set amount

00:07:15.820 --> 00:07:20.139
of force is applied, allowing the person to
move forward a bit more.

00:07:20.139 --> 00:07:22.870
Animation 7
There is also the torsion bar, a length of

00:07:22.870 --> 00:07:25.500
metal attached to the rotating spool.

00:07:25.500 --> 00:07:30.509
This torsion bar is designed to twist and
absorb some of the force from a crash, instead

00:07:30.509 --> 00:07:33.610
of allowing that energy to be transferred
to the passengers body..

00:07:33.610 --> 00:07:34.610
[9]

00:07:34.610 --> 00:07:39.139
Despite the clear benefits of this three-point
belt system, it took decades before the use

00:07:39.139 --> 00:07:42.060
of seatbelts really became widespread.

00:07:42.060 --> 00:07:46.310
Although Wisconsin was the first state to
require all cars to install seat belts in

00:07:46.310 --> 00:07:53.129
the front in 1961, it wasn’t until 1984
that another state, New York, required passengers

00:07:53.129 --> 00:07:54.790
to actually wear them.

00:07:54.790 --> 00:08:02.009
I remember growing in Ireland in the 90s and
seeing the change in opinions about seatbelts,

00:08:02.009 --> 00:08:06.789
and I especially remember moving to Malaysia,
a country with a terrible road safety record

00:08:06.789 --> 00:08:11.640
and where a friend of mine died because he
wasn’t wearing a seatbelt, and arguing with

00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:14.680
local co-workers who refused to wear them.

00:08:14.680 --> 00:08:21.349
The National Highway Traffic Safety Administration
estimates 15,000 lives are saved by seat belts

00:08:21.349 --> 00:08:25.970
every year in the U.S. alone, so please put
your seatbelts on.

00:08:25.970 --> 00:08:28.400
They do save lives.

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:32.219
Of course there are plenty of other technologies
helping to make cars safer.

00:08:32.219 --> 00:08:38.200
We have front and side airbags, crumple zones,
driver assist, and automatic emergency braking.

00:08:38.200 --> 00:08:43.039
And Volvo is now proposing a blanket-style
seat belt for its autonomous cars that would

00:08:43.039 --> 00:08:47.600
keep passengers safe and comfortable even
when they’re napping. [10] But that’s

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:52.620
still something of a pipe-dream, since we’re
still a ways away from self-driving cars.

00:08:52.620 --> 00:08:57.000
For now we are stuck with the three-point
harness, a simple and cheap device to keep

00:08:57.000 --> 00:08:58.500
passengers safe.

00:08:58.500 --> 00:09:02.019
Sometimes simple solutions really are the
most effective.

00:09:02.019 --> 00:09:05.200
Other times more complicated solutions are
needed.

00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:09.279
One concept is so complicated that I struggle
to wrap my head around it.

00:09:09.279 --> 00:09:15.220
I have a basic understanding of how computers
work, and a firm grasp of physics, but the

00:09:15.220 --> 00:09:18.570
physics of quantum computing goes right over
my head.

00:09:18.570 --> 00:09:24.200
I wanted to start learning more about it in
preparation for a future video, but my usual

00:09:24.200 --> 00:09:27.550
method of using college textbooks was not
helping me.

00:09:27.550 --> 00:09:34.150
I didn’t know what I didn’t know, which
makes asking the right questions really hard.

00:09:34.150 --> 00:09:40.140
Then I discovered that Brilliant has a new
course on quantum computing, created in partnership

00:09:40.140 --> 00:09:42.120
with quantum computing researchers.

00:09:42.120 --> 00:09:47.681
It took me from the very basics of quantum
computing, starting at a level I understand

00:09:47.681 --> 00:09:49.920
with basic logic gates.

00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:54.950
Testing my understanding along the way and
explaining to me the correct answers in clear

00:09:54.950 --> 00:09:57.730
understandable terms when I got something
wrong.

00:09:57.730 --> 00:10:02.940
I regularly have to dive into the deep end
of unfamiliar topics to write the scripts

00:10:02.940 --> 00:10:07.829
